
GRPN Version 1.0 

By Paul Wilkins

GRPN is a graphical RPN calculator built using Qt 6 (or Qt 5).

GRPN works with real numbers, complex numbers, matrices, and
complex matrices.  Numbers can be displayed in 4 different
radix modes, and complex numbers can be displayed in either
Cartesian or polar form.

Named variables can hold any calculator value. Type an apostrophe
followed by a name and press Sto to copy the top stack value into the
variable (for example, `'abc` then Sto). Enter the same name and press
Rcl to push a copy of the stored value back onto the stack, or Purge
to delete the variable. The first ten variables also appear in the
memory-button row above the keypad; clicking one of these buttons
recalls that variable. Additional variables remain available through
the Rcl command, and move into the memory-button row as earlier
variables are purged.

Variables are kept between runs in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/grpn/variables`
(normally `~/.config/grpn/variables`), which is rewritten each time a
variable is stored or purged. The C and Python versions share this
file. It is plain text, one variable per line; see `variables.h` for
the format.

GRPN is copyrighted under the terms of the GNU General Public
License.  See the file LICENSE for more details.


To build GRPN:

1) Install C and C++ compilers, make, pkg-config, the Qt Widgets
   development package (Qt 6, or Qt 5.11 or later), and the MPFR
   development package. On Debian/Ubuntu these are `qt6-base-dev` (or
   `qtbase5-dev`) and `libmpfr-dev`; on Fedora they are
   `qt6-qtbase-devel` (or `qt5-qtbase-devel`) and `mpfr-devel`.

   The calculator core is C and uses only MPFR; the user interface
   (main.cpp, lcd_qt.cpp, setup_buttons.cpp, setup_menu.cpp,
   options.cpp, help.cpp) is C++ and Qt.  gui.h is the interface
   between the two.

2) Run make.  This builds against Qt 6; to use Qt 5 instead, run

       make QT=5

   Switching QT between builds recompiles the Qt parts automatically.

3) Copy the executable, grpn, to its final installation directory,
   for example /usr/local/bin.

Linking Qt statically
---------------------

A grpn with Qt linked in runs on Linux systems that have no Qt
installed; it still uses the system's C library, X11/xcb libraries,
fontconfig, freetype, harfbuzz and MPFR.  Linux distributions do not
ship static Qt libraries, so build Qt yourself first.  For example,
with Qt 6 (from the qtbase source, qtbase-everywhere-src-6.x.y):

    sudo apt install cmake ninja-build libfontconfig-dev libfreetype-dev \
      libharfbuzz-dev libxkbcommon-dev libxkbcommon-x11-dev libx11-dev \
      libx11-xcb-dev libxcb1-dev libxcb-cursor-dev libxcb-icccm4-dev \
      libxcb-image0-dev libxcb-keysyms1-dev libxcb-randr0-dev \
      libxcb-render0-dev libxcb-render-util0-dev libxcb-shape0-dev \
      libxcb-shm0-dev libxcb-sync-dev libxcb-util-dev libxcb-xfixes0-dev \
      libxcb-xinerama0-dev libxcb-xinput-dev libxcb-xkb-dev
    mkdir build && cd build
    ../qtbase-everywhere-src-6.x.y/configure -static -release \
      -prefix /opt/qt6-static -nomake examples -nomake tests \
      -no-opengl -no-dbus -no-glib -no-icu -qt-zlib -qt-pcre \
      -qt-libpng -qt-libjpeg -system-freetype -system-harfbuzz \
      -fontconfig -xcb -no-feature-sql
    cmake --build . --parallel
    cmake --install .

(-no-dbus also turns off the X11 accessibility bridge.)  Then build
grpn against it:

    make QT_STATIC_PREFIX=/opt/qt6-static

A static Qt cannot load its platform plugins at run time, so the ones
grpn needs are linked in and registered when it is built.  By default
that is only the X11 plugin, xcb; on a Wayland desktop grpn then runs
through XWayland.  To choose others, list them in QT_STATIC_PLUGINS,
from xcb, wayland, offscreen and minimal:

    make QT_STATIC_PREFIX=/opt/qt6-static QT_STATIC_PLUGINS="xcb wayland"

The wayland plugin needs a Qt built with Wayland support, and a Qt
built as above has no window-decoration plugin, so on desktops that
leave decorations to the program (such as GNOME) grpn's window would
have no title bar.  qt-static-libs.sh reads the link dependencies from
the .prl files that a static Qt installs.  Changing QT, QT_STATIC_PREFIX
or QT_STATIC_PLUGINS rebuilds the Qt parts of grpn automatically.

To instead produce a fully static executable, install static archives
for Qt, MPFR, GMP, and all of their transitive dependencies (including
the X11 libraries), then run:

    make static          (or: make QT=5 static)

The result is `grpn-static`. The `static-check` target reports missing
primary archives before linking.

Python/PyQt5 version
--------------------

An equivalent Python implementation is provided in `grpn_py.py`. It uses
Python `Decimal` arithmetic at 77-digit precision and PyQt5 for the GUI.
It has the same keypad, keyboard input, typed commands, display, Options
window, variables and memory buttons, undo, and command-line options
(-fn, -btn-fn, -disp-fn, -rows, -cols, -b) as the C version. Install
PyQt5 and run it with:

    sudo apt install python3-pyqt5
    python3 grpn_py.py

The calculation engine can also be imported independently of the GUI:

    from grpn_py import Calculator, Matrix
    calc = Calculator()
    calc.type_text("2 3+")              # keys as typed; "\n" is Enter
    print(calc.format(calc.stack[-1]))   # 5
    lines, cursor = calc.display(30, 8)  # the display as text

Differences from the C version: the Python version shows the magnitude
of a complex zero as 0 rather than NaN, and labels the power keys x²,
yˣ, 10ˣ and eˣ.

GRPN uses MPFR with a default precision of 256 bits (approximately
77 decimal digits). The display shows 12 significant digits by default;
the Options window can change this from 1 to 70 digits. The Options
window can also switch to engineering notation (as on HP 48 series
calculators: exponent a multiple of 3, trailing zeros omitted, e.g.
12.345E3). Thousands separators are enabled by default and can
be disabled in the Options window. The Options window settings (base,
radix, coordinate mode, number formatting, displayed digits and display
font size) are saved on exit in ~/.config/grpn/grpn.conf (or
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/grpn/grpn.conf) and restored on the next run; grpn and
grpn_py.py share this file. The precision can be changed at compile time by
overriding `REAL_PRECISION_BITS` in real.h.



GRPN can be found at the following locations:
sunsite.unc.edu /pub/Linux/apps/math/calc
wilkins.ne.mediaone.net /pub


